rism, Economy and Communities Scrutiny  Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-12","committee":"Executive","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-04","committee":"Climate Change and Environment Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Executive","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Adult Social Care and Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-02","committee":"Children and Young People's Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-13","committee":"Executive","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Climate Change and Environment Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Adult Social Care and Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"DSG deficit of \u00a37.331m to be 90% written off by High Needs Stability Grant, leaving council to fund 10%","lead":"Partial bailout masks underlying structural high-needs funding gap; future DSG deficits uncertain and could resume accumulating if HNSG support is not confirmed","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Asset Disposal Plan is critical to financial position, required to cover \u00a36.663m Growth & Prosperity and \u00a34.966m Flexible Use of Capital Receipts targets","lead":"Primary mechanism for balancing the position depends on achieving significant asset sales; failure to deliver disposals would immediately create \u00a311.6m financing gap","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Council Tax collection rate 3rd lowest in England at 26% at month 3, reflecting deprivation, with recovery pressures mounting","lead":"Weak collection rates on main revenue source, with accumulated arrears and cost-of-living pressures suggesting continued headwinds to income recovery","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","headline":"DSG deficit of \u00a37.331m to be 90% written off by High Needs Stability Grant, leaving council to fund 10%","detail":"The Dedicated Schools Grant Reserve held a deficit of \u00a37.331m as at 31 March 2026. The Department for Education's High Needs Stability Grant will write off 90% of this accrued deficit, leaving the Council responsible for managing and funding the remaining 10% (approximately \u00a3733k). Future deficits accrued in 2026/27 onwards may be covered by future HNSG payments, but this is not yet confirmed by DfE as their review of High Needs funding continues.","category":"demand","matters":"Partial bailout masks underlying structural high-needs funding gap; future DSG deficits uncertain and could resume accumulating if HNSG support is not confirmed","figures":"\u00a37,331k DSG deficit at 31 March 2026; 90% written off by HNSG; Council responsible for \u00a3733k; future HNSG payments not yet confirmed","quote":"This one-off Grant will write-off 90% of the accrued DSG deficit as at 31 March 2026, with the Council responsible for managing and funding the remaining 10%. Deficits accrued in 2026/27 and beyond may be covered by future HNSG payments, but this is yet to be confirmed by the DfE","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","headline":"Asset Disposal Plan is critical to financial position, required to cover \u00a36.663m Growth & Prosperity and \u00a34.966m Flexible Use of Capital Receipts targets","detail":"Forecast Earmarked Revenue Reserves of \u00a321.34m assume that in-year Growth & Prosperity deficit (\u00a36.663m) and Flexible Use of Capital Receipts target (\u00a34.966m) are covered by the Asset Disposal Plan. An Asset Disposal working group has been established, meeting monthly, and is developing a contingency plan to mitigate delivery risk. Total of \u00a311.6m in required disposals underpins the current financial forecast.","category":"other","matters":"Primary mechanism for balancing the position depends on achieving significant asset sales; failure to deliver disposals would immediately create \u00a311.6m financing gap","figures":"\u00a36,663k G&P overspend dependent on Asset Disposal; \u00a34,966k Flexible Use of Capital Receipts dependent on Asset Disposal; total \u00a311.629m","quote":"This figure also assumes the in-year Growth & Prosperity (\u00a36.663m) and the Flexible Use of Capital Receipts target (\u00a34.966m) are covered by the Asset Disposal Plan. An Asset Disposal working group has been set up and is meeting monthly to progress these disposals","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","headline":"Council Tax collection rate 3rd lowest in England at 26% at month 3, reflecting deprivation, with recovery pressures mounting","detail":"Being the most deprived local authority in England, Council Tax collection is identified as a challenge. The 2025/26 collection rate dipped from 4th lowest to 3rd lowest in England. At end month 3, \u00a321.8m collected at 26% collection rate. Cumulative collection on arrears (2020/21 to 2025/26) at 91.4%. The Council Tax Reduction Scheme collection is under 'similar pressure' due to accumulated arrears and cost of living impact on household income.","category":"income","matters":"Weak collection rates on main revenue source, with accumulated arrears and cost-of-living pressures suggesting continued headwinds to income recovery","figures":"26.0% collection rate at month 3; 3rd lowest in England for 2025/26; cumulative arrears collection rate 91.4%; CTRS collection rate 20.8%","quote":"Being the most deprived local authority in England per the Office for National Statistics, collection of Council Tax is a challenge and 2025/26 saw the collection rate dip from 4th lowest in England to 3rd lowest","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","headline":"Council borrowing nearly doubled from \u00a3250m (2017/18) to \u00a3587m (2025/26) with 50:50 split between long and short-term at rates exceeding 6%","detail":"Council borrowing has grown from less than \u00a3250m at end 2017/18 to \u00a3587m at end 2025/26, forecast to \u00a3605m. The borrowing profile changed to 50:50 long-term and short-term split as at 2025/26, after shorter 'long-term' borrowing was taken in 2025/26. Long-term PWLB rates currently exceed 6% despite base rate at 3.75%. Month 3 2026/27 shows long-term borrowing increased by \u00a328.4m.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Doubling of borrowing stock combined with elevated long-term rates and shift to shorter-term debt increases refinancing and interest rate risk exposure","figures":"\u00a3587m at 2025/26; forecast \u00a3605m for 2026/27; long-term rates >6%; short-term borrowing increased \u00a328.4m in first 3 months","quote":"Council borrowing has doubled from less than \u00a3250m at the end of 2017/18 to \u00a3587m at the end of 2025/26. The profile of this borrowing has also changed to a 50:50 split of long-term and short-term borrowing as at 2025/26","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","headline":"Strategic Leisure Assets (Tower and Winter Gardens) forecast \u00a31.721m overspend with cumulative deficit expected to reach \u00a312.474m","detail":"Strategic Leisure Assets is forecasting a \u00a31.721m pressure in 2026/27. Cumulative deficit expected to reach \u00a312.474m by 31 March 2027. The council notes that under the original 2011 Executive decision, any over/underspend is transferred to an Earmarked Reserve. A new leisure assets medium-term financial plan is being worked up following management change (creation of Blackpool Tourism Limited). The previous plan expected to break-even cumulatively by 2034/35.","category":"capital","matters":"Long-term cultural/tourism investment with significant accumulated deficit and uncertain breakeven timeline; dependent on Blackpool Tourism Limited's commercial performance","figures":"\u00a31,721k forecast pressure; \u00a312,474k cumulative deficit forecast at 31 March 2027; previous MTFP expected breakeven by 2034/35","quote":"Strategic Leisure Assets, which largely relates to the investment in key historic and economic assets of Blackpool Tower and the Winter Gardens, forecast a pressure of \u00a31,721k... cumulative deficit as at 31 March 2027 will be \u00a312,474k","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-14"},"name":"Blackpool UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-20","since_iso":"2026-08-21","distress":true,"headline":"DSG deficit of \u00a37.331m to be 90% written off by High Needs Stability Grant, leaving council to fund 10%","lead":"Partial bailout masks underlying structural high-needs funding gap; future DSG deficits uncertain and could resume accumulating if HNSG support is not confirmed","also":["46 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":47,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Item 3 Financial Performance Monitoring M3 2627","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.blackpool.gov.uk/documents/s111521/111521.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":92,"news_drafts_total":3}}